<PageLayout
  title="Code"
  description="Engineering work supporting the Open Small Models Accord — current and planned."
  lede="Open infrastructure for running, evaluating, and composing small models. The work is in three pieces: a runtime toolkit for mobile devices, a disclosure schema, and a public registry that records what is open and what is not.">

  <h2>mobile-ai-toolkit</h2>
  <p>
    A toolkit for running small language models on mobile and other constrained hardware,
    with a focus on local-first inference, deterministic action boundaries, and
    inspectable runtime behavior.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> active development.
    <a href="https://github.com/openslm-ai/mobile-ai-toolkit">Repository</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>LWD-R schema</h2>
  <p>
    A machine-readable schema for describing a release's openness across the four layers
    of the accord — Logic, Weights, Data, and Representation. Each release publishes a
    disclosure document; the schema defines what the disclosure must contain so that
    independent parties can compare claims across releases.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> planned. Repository link forthcoming.
  </p>

  <h2>Public registry</h2>
  <p>
    A registry that collects LWD-R disclosures from signatories and renders them as a
    public, queryable record. The registry is the mechanism by which endorsement becomes
    something that can be checked rather than asserted.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Status:</strong> planned. Repository link forthcoming.
  </p>
</PageLayout>
